#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559908} :activity/id #uuid "d39c546e-aa9b-57f9-9da7-aa0899e1ca37" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050100 :topic/id #uuid "2d1d35b9-13b1-5815-b3ec-f38b609cdc4c" :topic/math-academy-id 1213} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fbea327-41aa-5fdc-a977-23866bec8887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559179} {:answer/id #uuid "534e43c0-1360-5b0d-8a4a-5d2bde094e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$5$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559228} {:answer/id #uuid "f00913c9-54b0-51ef-a4b0-ef296a0170c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559598} {:answer/id #uuid "f0dc00a2-c124-5ff2-b78f-80d14094484c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$15$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559603} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a02da5-372b-5869-879e-c7b98972e685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559228} :answer-field/id #uuid "907188cd-1b8a-5efd-a8a3-c00dedfb9afa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bae7b49c-3856-514a-a9ab-b99ea914cf24" :question/math-academy-id "q-117054" :question/problem "![](images/2c/2cfba1072b37cd316349997201ada6e67af702926214abec22a1ef5ae6543c5a.png)\n\nConsider a box with a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The volume of the box is $125$ cubic units. Given that the surface area of the box has been minimized, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2{x}^{2}+4xy\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $125$ cubic units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 125 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =125 \\\\ y & =\\frac{125}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2{x}^{2}+4xy \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+4x\\cdot \\frac{125}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+\\frac{500}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=4x-\\frac{500}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-\\frac{500}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{500}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =500 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=4+\\frac{1\\,000}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=4+8=12>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed a minimum of $S(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the smallest possible surface area has side length $x=5$ units."} :step/id #uuid "2470c84c-c97b-513d-8e62-311c5ead05dc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd9f3f04-dec2-59a4-90c7-4e80640a9ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559638} :answer-field/id #uuid "41fc7962-a273-588e-89ef-1ba97b8bff24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "35d621eb-cf33-51a5-9f2c-6f01d51e0a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-347273" :question/problem "![](images/12/12a9961800e0578de92b428e1a1a6ba5e10e25fe635a90f85c772f9bf73dc997.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $15\\,cm\\times 7\\,cm.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{cm}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height $y$ of the box that gives the maximum possible volume.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=15-2y,\\,z=7-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $15-2y>0$ and $7-2y>0$, hence $y<\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(15-2y)(y)(7-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-44{y}^{2}+105y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-88y+105,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-88y+105 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{35}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $\\frac{35}{6}$ since we already know that $0<y<\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{3}{2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-88.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{3}{2}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the height of the box with the maximum volume is $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\text{ cm}.$"} :step/id #uuid "3eaa5e01-60b9-5696-a1e7-991044c7bbba"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26d46271-3014-5476-b936-8ff5de06d051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559112} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ae05d3c-21d9-5363-8872-60ad0b3f9a59" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "88d64cef-5be0-59d0-8f76-951d4350e51b" :question/math-academy-id "q-347083" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d67ba70d92807f0a7298fb8222252a047f7eec03a26878a5aa95b0fb82a7335c.png)\n\nA box with no lid whose depth $z$ is $3$ times its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$2$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $972\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2xz+1\\cdot 2xy+1\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =2xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $972\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 972 & =x\\cdot z\\cdot (\\frac{z}{3}) \\\\ 972 & =\\frac{1}{3}x{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x{z}^{2} & =972 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2\\,(\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}})\\,z+2\\,(\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}})\\,(\\frac{z}{3})+2\\,(\\frac{z}{3})\\,z \\\\  & =\\frac{5832}{z}+\\frac{1944}{z}+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7776}{z}+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=\\frac{4}{3}z-\\frac{7776}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}z-\\frac{7776}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{z}^{3} & =7776 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =5832 \\\\ z & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=18$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{15552}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(18)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=4>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(18)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=18$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=18$ units."} :step/id #uuid "83c4667d-a7a5-57bc-aaae-222f06c3f1de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02932fbd-ee3d-58bf-9d40-e6c4b5772ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559022} {:answer/id #uuid "0653430e-b3f6-5da7-8b4a-42f0139025c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559029} {:answer/id #uuid "13d2aacc-5287-5151-8fa4-4b990a54d3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{7}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559061} {:answer/id #uuid "2c37baac-a6d7-52ff-b039-36b9f3d4538b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5}{3}}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559127} {:answer/id #uuid "72638d95-8910-5c23-aa5c-c06b64acc003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559302} :answer-field/id #uuid "76985055-fc94-55ff-998a-6e253b938977" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8973c685-fd2c-51e0-89ff-171e4339d4cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-117217" :question/problem "![](images/af/afc68dbb5c336cff31e75f5ed92dd853c8fc7ec43e8fa66539d27d83b9e2070f.png)\n\nA box with no lid and a square base has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$3$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$5$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $2\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ for the box that has the smallest production cost?" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =3{x}^{2}+5\\cdot 4xy \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+20xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $2\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 2 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =3{x}^{2}+20xy \\\\ C & =3{x}^{2}+20x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ C & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{40}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=6x-\\frac{40}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 6x-\\frac{40}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3} & =40 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=6+\\frac{80}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}})=6+12=18>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}}$ is indeed a minimum of $C(x).$"} :step/id #uuid "986d328c-4039-5272-94a8-a5b54b4004c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559960}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559960 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3088ae4-d68a-57e8-b0d8-0c5b606e66de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "648" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559463} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d8dbeb-a278-5ecd-a7ab-cdc1d19cdc10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3b6389e0-fab8-5e1d-bb5a-1e8d075edcaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-346784" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4cafc2891cfd957aad10e876d51757e8c6d7e84844927cb57ce1c2e704fe5b55.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is three times its width. Given that the box's volume is $972$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $972$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 972 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=3x,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n972=3{x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y & =972 \\\\ y & =\\frac{324}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(3x)+2x(\\frac{324}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{324}{{x}^{2}})(3x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+\\frac{648}{x}+\\frac{1944}{x} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+\\frac{2592}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=12x-\\frac{2592}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12x-\\frac{2592}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{3}-2592 & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{3} & =2592 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =216 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=6$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=12+\\frac{5184}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(6)=12+24=36>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(6)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=6$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(6) & =6(6)^{2}+\\frac{2592}{6} \\\\  & =648\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "afee5c19-21de-5dac-b2a9-ef66458e0dec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559946}}] :activity/title "Optimization Problems Involving Boxes and Trays" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559011}]}
